29329:
Convert to kelvin with T_1 = 300 K and T_2 = 400 K, since v_{rms} = \sqrt{\frac{3 R T}{M}} scales as \sqrt{T}
The ratio is \frac{v_2}{v_1} = \sqrt{\frac{400}{300}} = \sqrt{\frac{4}{3}} = \frac{2}{\sqrt{3}}
This equals option A → (A)

29330:
For a simple pendulum T = 2 \pi \sqrt{\frac{L}{g}}, so the period scales as \sqrt{L}
Raising the length from L to 4 L multiplies the period by \sqrt{4} = 2
Hence T' = 2 \times 2 s = 4 s, which is option A → (A)

29334:
Potential adds as scalars with V = \frac{1}{4 \pi \varepsilon_0} (\frac{q}{r_1} - \frac{q}{r_2})
Every point (0, y) is equidistant from the two charges, so r_1 = r_2 = \sqrt{a^2 + y^2}
The two terms cancel and V = 0 everywhere on the y-axis, option A → (A)

29335:
Angular fringe width is \theta = \frac{\lambda}{d}
With \lambda = 500 \times 10^{-9} m and d = 10^{-3} m, \theta = \frac{500 \times 10^{-9}}{10^{-3}} = 5 \times 10^{-4} \text{ rad}
This is option A → (A)

29336:
Each side is at perpendicular distance a / 2 from the centre, subtending 45^\circ at each end
One side gives B_1 = \frac{\mu_0 I}{4 \pi (a / 2)} (\sin 45^\circ + \sin 45^\circ) = \frac{\mu_0 I}{\pi a \sqrt{2}}
Four identical sides add to B = \frac{2 \sqrt{2} \mu_0 I}{\pi a}, option A → (A)

29339:
Open pipe fundamental is f = \frac{v}{2 L_1} while closed pipe fundamental is f = \frac{v}{4 L_2}
Equal frequency gives \frac{v}{2 L_1} = \frac{v}{4 L_2}
Hence \frac{L_1}{L_2} = 2, option A → (A)

29342:
Close-orbit speed satisfies \frac{m v_0^2}{R} = m g, giving v_0 = \sqrt{g R}
Escape needs \frac{1}{2} m v_e^2 = m g R, giving v_e = \sqrt{2 g R} = \sqrt{2} v_0
So escape velocity is \sqrt{2} v_0, option A → (A)

29343:
Isothermal work for n moles is W = n R T \ln \frac{V_2}{V_1}
Here n = 1 and V_2 / V_1 = 2, so W = R T \ln 2
This is option A → (A)

29344:
The concave lens has f = -20 cm and forms a diminished erect image with m = \frac{v}{u} = +\frac{1}{3}
Lens formula \frac{1}{v} - \frac{1}{u} = \frac{1}{f} with v = u / 3 gives u = -40 cm
Object distance is therefore 40 cm, option A → (A)

29346:
The two dielectric fillings sit side by side, so they add in parallel to C = 15 pF
Series combination with 7.5 pF gives C_{eq} = \frac{15 \times 7.5}{15 + 7.5} = \frac{112.5}{22.5} = 5 \text{ pF}
The equivalent capacitance is therefore 5 pF → (5)

29349:
Resonance is at \omega_0 = \frac{1}{\sqrt{L C}}
With L = 0.1 H and C = 10 \times 10^{-6} F, L C = 10^{-6} \text{ s}^2
So \omega_0 = \frac{1}{\sqrt{10^{-6}}} = 1000 \text{ rad / s} → (1000)

29350:
Displacement is \Delta \vec{r} = \vec{r}_2 - \vec{r}_1 = 2 \hat{i} + 3 \hat{j} + 2 \hat{k} \text{ m}
Work is W = \vec{F} \cdot \Delta \vec{r} = 2 \times 2 + 3 \times 3 + 4 \times 2 = 4 + 9 + 8
So W = 21 \text{ J} → (21)

29355:
A conjugate acid is produced by adding one proton to the base
\text{NH}_{3} + \text{H}^{+} \rightarrow \text{NH}_{4}^{+}
The protonated product is the ammonium ion, which is the first listed option → (A)

29368:
\text{Mn}^{2+} is formed by losing the 4s electrons, leaving the configuration 3d^{5}
Unpaired electron count is therefore n = 5
Spin-only moment is \mu = \sqrt{n(n + 2)} = \sqrt{5 \times 7} = \sqrt{35}
Evaluation gives \mu = 5.92 BM → (A)

29376:
Recall that composition means substituting f into itself, so f(f(x)) = \frac{f(x) - 1}{f(x) + 1}
Insert f(x) = \frac{x - 1}{x + 1} to get f(f(x)) = \frac{\frac{x - 1}{x + 1} - 1}{\frac{x - 1}{x + 1} + 1}
Simplify the numerator as \frac{x - 1}{x + 1} - 1 = \frac{x - 1 - x - 1}{x + 1} = \frac{-2}{x + 1}
Simplify the denominator as \frac{x - 1}{x + 1} + 1 = \frac{x - 1 + x + 1}{x + 1} = \frac{2x}{x + 1}
Dividing cancels the common factor x + 1 and leaves \frac{-2}{2x}
Reducing the fraction gives -1/x, which matches option A → (A)

29377:
Factor the numerator using \tan x - \sin x = \tan x (1 - \cos x)
Rewrite the quotient as \frac{\tan x}{x} \times \frac{1 - \cos x}{x^2}
Recall the standard limits \lim_{x \to 0} \frac{\tan x}{x} = 1 and \lim_{x \to 0} \frac{1 - \cos x}{x^2} = \frac{1}{2}
For the second limit write 1 - \cos x = 2 \sin^2 \frac{x}{2}, so the ratio equals \frac{1}{2} \left( \frac{\sin \frac{x}{2}}{\frac{x}{2}} \right)^2 \to \frac{1}{2}
Multiplying the limits gives 1 \times \frac{1}{2} = \frac{1}{2}
This value matches option A → (A)

29380:
Find the intersections by putting y = \frac{x^2}{4} into y^2 = 4x, giving \frac{x^4}{16} = 4x
Solve x^4 = 64x to get x = 0 and x = 4, so the curves meet at (0, 0) and (4, 4)
Between them the upper curve is y = 2\sqrt{x} and the lower one is y = \frac{x^2}{4}
Set up Area = \int_0^4 \left( 2\sqrt{x} - \frac{x^2}{4} \right) dx
Integrate to \left[ \frac{4x^{3/2}}{3} - \frac{x^3}{12} \right]_0^4 = \frac{32}{3} - \frac{16}{3} = \frac{16}{3}
This value matches option A → (A)

29386:
Separate the variables as \frac{dy}{y} = \frac{dx}{x}
Integrate both sides to get \int \frac{dy}{y} = \int \frac{dx}{x}
Evaluate the logarithms as \ln|y| = \ln|x| + C_1
Exponentiate and absorb the constant by setting e^{C_1} = C
Arrive at y = Cx, which matches option A → (A)

29392:
Use the amplitude bound for a \sin x + b \cos x, whose maximum equals \sqrt{a^2 + b^2}
Justify it by writing 3 \sin x + 4 \cos x = R \sin(x + \phi) with R = \sqrt{3^2 + 4^2}
Here a = 3 and b = 4, so R = \sqrt{9 + 16}
Evaluate \sqrt{25} = 5, attained when \sin(x + \phi) = 1
This maximum matches option A → (A)

29393:
Each term of (x + y + z)^{10} is x^i y^j z^k with i + j + k = 10
Count the exponent triples by stars and bars: \binom{10 + 3 - 1}{3 - 1} = \binom{12}{2}
Expand the binomial coefficient as \frac{12 \times 11}{2}
Evaluate to get 66
This count matches option A → (A)
